---
const repos = [
  { n: 'api', v: 'v4', c: 'ok' },
  { n: 'web', v: 'v3', c: 'warn' },
  { n: 'billing', v: 'main', c: 'bad', at: true },
  { n: 'auth', v: 'v4', c: 'ok' },
  { n: 'search', v: 'v2', c: 'warn' },
  { n: 'mobile', v: 'v3', c: 'warn' },
  { n: 'infra', v: 'main', c: 'bad', at: true },
  { n: 'docs', v: 'v4', c: 'ok' },
  { n: 'data', v: '8e5e7a2…', c: 'ok' },
];
const secrets = Array.from({ length: 9 }, (_, i) => ({ n: repos[i]!.n, fresh: i === 2 || i === 6 }));
---
<section id="problem" class="sec" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
    <h2 id="problem-title">Your CI is already a fleet.<br />It just isn't managed like one.</h2>
    <p class="sec-lede">
      Every repo got its workflow by copying the last one. Then each copy lived its own life.
    </p>

    <div class="prob-grid">
      <article class="card" aria-labelledby="p1">
        <h3 id="p1">Nine repos, one intent, five spellings</h3>
        <div class="repo-grid" data-anim id="drift" role="img"
          aria-label="Nine repositories all containing the same checkout step, which has drifted into five different refs: v4, v3, v2, main, and a pinned sha">
          {repos.map((r, i) => (
            <div class="repo" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="repo-name">acme/{r.n}</span>
              <code class="repo-line">uses: actions/checkout@<span class="vstack" style={`--d:${700 + i * 260}`}><span class="v v-old ok">v4</span><span class={`v v-new ${r.c}`}>{r.v}</span></span></code>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <ul class="legend" aria-hidden="true">
          <li><i class="dot ok"></i>current / pinned</li>
          <li><i class="dot warn"></i>outdated tag</li>
          <li><i class="dot bad"></i>floating branch</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note">Nobody decided this. It's what copy-paste does over two years. Updating means opening nine PRs by hand, so it doesn't happen.</p>
        <button class="replay" type="button" data-replay="drift">Replay</button>
      </article>

      <article class="card" aria-labelledby="p2">
        <h3 id="p2">A secret rotated in 2 of 9 repos</h3>
        <div class="chips" data-anim id="rot" role="img"
          aria-label="Nine repositories hold the same NPM_TOKEN secret. After a rotation, only two of them have the new value; seven still hold the old one.">
          {secrets.map((s, i) => (
            <div class="chip" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="chip-name">{s.n}</span>
              <span class="vstack" style={`--d:${900 + i * 120}`}>
                <span class={`v v-old bad${s.fresh ? '' : ' keep'}`}>old key</span>
                {s.fresh && <span class="v v-new ok">rotated</span>}
              </span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="note">Rotation is a ticket per repo, tracked in someone's spreadsheet. Seven repos keep running on the key you meant to retire.</p>
        <button class="replay" type="button" data-replay="rot">Replay</button>
      </article>

      <article class="card card-wide" aria-labelledby="p3">
        <h3 id="p3">A moving tag is a supply-chain hole</h3>
        <div class="tag-story" data-anim id="tag" role="img"
          aria-label="Diagram: the version tag v3 of a third-party action initially points to a reviewed commit, then is re-pointed to a malicious commit. A workflow using @v3 runs the malicious commit; a workflow pinned to the reviewed sha keeps running the reviewed commit.">
          <div class="ts-col" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="ts-tag st" style="--d:200">tag <b>v3</b></div>
            <svg class="ts-arrow" viewBox="0 0 120 110" width="120" height="110"><path class="a1" d="M0 55 C 50 55, 60 17, 108 17" /><path class="ah1" d="M100 11 l9 6 -9 6" /><path class="a2" d="M0 55 C 50 55, 60 91, 108 91" /><path class="ah2" d="M100 85 l9 6 -9 6" /></svg>
            <div class="ts-commits">
              <div class="ts-commit good st" style="--d:200"><code>a1b2c3d…</code><span>reviewed</span></div>
              <div class="ts-commit evil st" style="--d:1500"><code>f00dfac…</code><span>malicious</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ts-col ts-wf" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="wf st" style="--d:1900"><code>uses: some/action@<b>v3</b></code><span class="bad-t">runs f00dfac…</span></div>
            <div class="wf st" style="--d:2150"><code>uses: some/action@<b>a1b2c3d…</b> # v3</code><span class="ok-t">still runs a1b2c3d…</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          Illustrative SHAs. This is not hypothetical: in March 2025 the widely used
          <code>tj-actions/changed-files</code> action was compromised and its existing version tags were re-pointed at a commit that
          printed secrets into workflow logs (CVE-2025-30066). Workflows pinned to a full commit SHA kept running the commit they had been reviewed against.
          The fix is old advice, pin by SHA. The problem is doing it, and <em>keeping the pins current</em>, in every repo by hand.
        </p>
        <button class="replay" type="button" data-replay="tag">Replay</button>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
